-- title:
--   Integrally closed R[j]-integral subalgebras equal the finite chart algebra
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring, $F$ a field equipped with an $R$-algebra structure, and $j$ a nonzero element of $F$. Let $B$ be an $R$-subalgebra of $F$ satisfying three conditions: $j \in B$; every $x \in B$ is integral over the $R$-subalgebra $\mathrm{Algebra.adjoin}\ R\ \{j\} = R[j]$ of $F$ generated by $j$; and $B$ is integrally closed in $F$, in the sense that every $x \in F$ which is integral over $B$ already lies in $B$. The conclusion is that $B$ equals `chartAlgFin R F j`, that is `chartAlg R F {j}`: the $R$-subalgebra of $F$ whose underlying set is $\{x \in F : x \text{ is integral over } R[j]\}$ (closed under the ring operations because sums and products of integral elements are integral, and containing the image of $R$). No finiteness or Noetherian hypothesis on $R$, $B$ or the extension is assumed, and nothing is asserted about the scheme [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236) itself; the statement is purely about the subalgebra $B$ of $F$.
--
--   This identifies the integral closure of $R[j]$ in $F$ — the algebra of the $j$-finite chart of the two-chart integral model — as the unique $R[j]$-integral $R$-subalgebra of $F$ that is integrally closed in $F$: any such intermediate order is already the whole chart algebra. It is used to recognise the chart algebra in the modular-curve setting, being cited by the statements identifying the range of the classifying map for full level structures with `chartAlgFin` (in the $\Gamma_0$-power and the Diamond rigid-data variants).
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel_eq_chartAlgFin_of_isIntegral_of_integrallyClosed.lean

